{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Who has counted the dust of Jacob</b> — like עֹפֶר הָאַיָּלִים (the young of the hinds), on account of \"For Israel is a youth, and I loved him\" etc. And so Onkelos translated: מִן יְכוֹל לְמִמְנֵי דְעַדְיָא דְבֵית יַעֲקֹב (who could count the multitude of the house of Jacob)." "<b>עָפָר</b> in gematria עַרְלָה in chol — a hint to what they give the עֲדָלָה in chol." "<b>תָמוּת</b> — three times in the Masorah: here, and the other: \"Let me die with the Philistines\" (Judges 16:30), and the other: \"Their soul dies in youth\" (Psalm 17:14). He asked that his soul die with the upright, but it was not so; rather, the opposite: he died the death of Philistines, i.e., the death of the wicked, and in youth, as is stated: \"Men of blood and deceit shall not halve their days\" (Psalm 55:24) — for he was thirty-four years old when Phinehas killed him." "<b>יְשָׁרִים</b> in gematria: the fathers of the world. The final letters of the names of the fathers in gematria: בִּלְעָם. And this is: \"My end shall be like him\" (Num 24:14)."], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The Hebrew text has עופר in the first comparison and עפר in the lemma; both translated as 'dust/young' per context. The Onkelos translation is given in Aramaic script in the original.", 1 "The terms ערלה, חול, and עדלה are retained in Hebrew script as they are technical/vernacular terms whose precise referent (orlah-dough, chol-offering, adalah-portions) is debated; the argument requires them untranslated. The gematria of עפר = ערלה = 321.", 2 "The three Masorah occurrences are תמות (Num 23:10), תמות נפשי עם פלשתים (Judg 16:30), and תמות בנוער נפשם (Ps 17:14). The final clause's subject (Phinehas killing him at 34) refers to Balaam, per rabbinic tradition; no subject is added beyond what the text implies.", 3 "The 'final letters of the names of the fathers' (סופי תיבות של שמות האבות) is a notarikon: taking the last letters of אברהם יצחק יעקב, and their gematria equals בלעם = 142. The concluding quotation ותהי אחריתי כמוהו is from Numbers 24:14 but carries no verse address in the original."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:23:08.594701982Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}